1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,930
At twenty-five past one in the morning,

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:05,650
a ship as long as three football
pitches went completely dark.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,370
Four minutes later, a
bridge that had stood

4
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:11,490
for forty-seven years
was lying in the river.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:14,590
Six men working on that
bridge never went home.

6
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:18,030
And when investigators finally
found what started it all,

7
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,010
it was a piece of plastic
smaller than your finger.

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,790
So how does a tiny label
take down a giant bridge?

9
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,330
To understand that, we need to go back

10
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:28,810
to that night, and on board that ship.

11
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:30,570
The ship is called Dali.

12
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:33,350
It's a container ship,
three hundred metres long,

13
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:35,770
built in South Korea in twenty fifteen.

14
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:38,730
On the night of the twenty-sixth
of March, twenty twenty-four,

15
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:42,070
it's leaving the Port of
Baltimore, in the United States.

16
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:44,990
It's carrying about four thousand
seven hundred containers,

17
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,870
and it's heading for Sri Lanka.

18
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:51,610
On board are twenty-one crew,
and two local harbour pilots,

19
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,110
experts who guide big
ships through the harbour.

20
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:57,890
In a big port, ships don't
find their own way in and out.

21
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,870
Every harbour has its own bends,
shallow spots and bridges,

22
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:04,630
so local pilots come on board and give the

23
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:07,810
steering orders until the
ship reaches open water.

24
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,990
Ahead of it is the
Francis Scott Key Bridge.

25
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:13,330
It's about two point six kilometres long,

26
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,750
and it carries a busy
highway across the river.

27
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,630
And it has a famous name.

28
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:20,890
In eighteen fourteen,
Francis Scott Key watched a

29
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:23,370
battle at Fort McHenry,
just up this same river,

30
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:26,850
and wrote the poem that became
America's national anthem.

31
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:30,390
Every summer, a red,
white and blue buoy is

32
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:33,070
placed in the water near
the spot where he watched.

33
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:36,470
And on the bridge that
night, a small road crew is

34
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:39,330
doing a normal night-shift
job: filling potholes.

35
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:40,710
Everything is normal.

36
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:41,770
Until it isn't.

37
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:45,530
At twenty-five past one, when
the ship is only about three ship

38
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:49,230
lengths away from the bridge,
every light on the Dali goes out.

39
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:51,890
To understand why that's so dangerous,

40
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:54,050
you need to know one
thing about big ships.

41
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:56,490
A modern ship is basically
a floating power station.

42
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:57,990
Generators make the electricity,

43
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:01,270
and that electricity
runs almost everything.

44
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:02,190
The steering.

45
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:03,110
The lights.

46
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:03,690
The radios.

47
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:06,530
And the pumps that keep
the main engine alive.

48
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:08,730
The main engine is cooled by water,

49
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:11,850
and that water is pushed
around by electric pumps.

50
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:14,030
So when the power dies, the cooling stops,

51
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:16,550
and the main engine shuts itself down.

52
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:20,030
No engine means no propeller
pushing water past the rudder.

53
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:23,170
And without that flow, the
rudder can barely steer.

54
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,710
So why not just stop?

55
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:27,430
Because a ship this size
can't stop like a car.

56
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:28,610
There are no brakes.

57
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:32,130
When it's fully loaded, the Dali
and its cargo weigh well over

58
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:35,650
a hundred thousand tonnes, and
all of that keeps gliding forward.

59
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:39,730
Even at a walking pace, that
much weight doesn't want to stop.

60
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,810
Imagine trying to stop a sliding
block of flats with your hands.

61
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,010
The pilots try everything they can.

62
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,170
They order the rudder
hard over to turn away,

63
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:50,630
and they drop an anchor to
drag the ship and slow it down.

64
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:52,870
But an anchor isn't a parking brake.

65
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:55,470
It digs in and drags, and that takes time.

66
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,450
Three ship lengths is not much time.

67
00:02:57,720 --> 00:02:59,790
The crew get some power
back within about a minute.

68
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:02,290
And then, it goes out again.

69
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:06,130
At twenty-seven minutes past one,
a mayday goes out on the radio.

70
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:08,870
The ship has lost power, and
it's heading for the bridge.

71
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:11,810
What happened next probably
saved a lot of lives.

72
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:13,030
The message reached the police,

73
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:16,530
and officers at both ends of
the bridge stopped the traffic.

74
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:18,450
They had about ninety seconds.

75
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:22,930
And in that time, no more
cars drove onto the bridge.

76
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:25,010
One officer even radioed that he would

77
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:27,430
drive onto the bridge
to warn the road crew.

78
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:29,350
There wasn't enough time.

79
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,650
Later, the NTSB said the
quick actions of the pilots,

80
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,410
the dispatchers and the police
prevented a much bigger loss of life.

81
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:39,970
But the road crew in the middle
didn't get the warning in time.

82
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:44,250
At about one twenty-nine, the
Dali, moving at around seven knots,

83
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:47,750
a fast walking pace, hits one
of the bridge's main supports.

84
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:50,470
The bridge comes down
in about thirty seconds.

85
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:53,650
Two people were rescued from
the water and the wreckage.

86
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:55,770
Six road workers died.

87
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:58,090
Divers went into the
river that same night.

88
00:03:58,240 --> 00:03:59,570
The water was dark and cold,

89
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:02,450
and full of twisted steel
and broken concrete.

90
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:04,290
The search was slow and dangerous.

91
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:06,830
Some of the wreckage was
so tangled that divers

92
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,110
couldn't reach it until
salvage crews had cut it apart.

93
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:12,930
Two of the workers were
found the next day,

94
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:15,230
inside their truck at
the bottom of the river.

95
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:17,890
The others were found
over the following weeks.

96
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:21,050
The last of the six was
recovered on the seventh of May.

97
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:22,750
Six weeks after the bridge fell.

98
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:24,910
So how can one hit bring down a bridge

99
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:27,190
that's more than two kilometres long?

100
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:30,530
The middle part of the Key Bridge
was a continuous steel truss,

101
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:32,910
one long piece resting on a few supports.

102
00:04:33,060 --> 00:04:34,410
Take away one of those supports,

103
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:36,930
and the long piece can't hold itself up.

104
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:38,190
It all goes together.

105
00:04:38,340 --> 00:04:40,530
Big bridges over shipping
lanes are protected by

106
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:43,290
barriers around their
supports, called dolphins.

107
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:44,870
They're there to catch or push away

108
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,770
a ship before it reaches the bridge.

109
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:51,310
On the newest bridges, the supports
even sit on artificial islands of rock,

110
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:55,130
so a ship runs aground long
before it can touch the bridge.

111
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:57,950
But the Key Bridge was built
in the nineteen-seventies.

112
00:04:58,020 --> 00:04:59,950
Ships have become much bigger since then,

113
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,410
and its protection was far too
small for a ship like the Dali.

114
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:07,130
Later, the investigators
calculated that the risk of this

115
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:10,430
bridge collapsing after a ship
strike was almost thirty times

116
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:13,270
higher than what's acceptable
for a bridge this important.

117
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:15,270
And nobody had ever checked.

118
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:17,150
And here's the frustrating part.

119
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:20,850
America had already learned
this lesson once, the hard way.

120
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:24,170
It's the ninth of May, nineteen
eighty, in Tampa Bay, Florida.

121
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,870
Early in the morning, a
sudden storm hits the bay.

122
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:28,810
A cargo ship called the Summit Venture

123
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:31,310
is heading for the Sunshine Skyway Bridge.

124
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,630
In the storm, the crew can barely
see, and the ship's radar fails.

125
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,730
The ship hits one of
the bridge's supports.

126
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:40,170
A long section of the
bridge falls into the bay.

127
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:43,730
Cars and a bus drove off the
broken edge in the storm.

128
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:45,450
Thirty-five people died.

129
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,520
One man survived.

130
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:48,450
His pickup truck fell with the road,

131
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:50,390
hit the side of the ship, and sank.

132
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:51,650
He swam back to the surface,

133
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:54,590
and the ship's own crew
pulled him out of the water.

134
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:56,830
That disaster changed the rules.

135
00:05:57,060 --> 00:05:59,830
After it, engineers in the
United States created a

136
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:02,730
way to calculate how likely a
bridge is to be hit by a ship,

137
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:05,250
and how strong its protection needs to be.

138
00:06:05,460 --> 00:06:07,690
The new Sunshine Skyway
was built with huge

139
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:09,510
protective barriers around its supports.

140
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:12,130
But those rules were made for new bridges.

141
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:14,070
The Key Bridge had been built before them.

142
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,070
And nobody went back to check it against

143
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:18,170
the bigger and bigger
ships coming through.

144
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:21,370
The United States safety board, the NTSB,

145
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:23,690
investigated the Dali for twenty months.

146
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:26,410
Their final report came out in
November twenty twenty-five.

147
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:29,570
And the first blackout
came down to one wire.

148
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:31,430
Inside an electrical cabinet,

149
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:34,170
there's a strip of terminals
where wires are clamped in.

150
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,930
Each wire has a small plastic
label wrapped around it,

151
00:06:36,980 --> 00:06:39,530
so engineers know which wire is which.

152
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:41,990
On one wire, that label had been put on

153
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,070
in the wrong place when
the ship was built.

154
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:46,930
It stopped the wire from going
all the way into its slot.

155
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:48,850
So the wire was only half connected.

156
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,350
For years it held.

157
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:52,670
Then that night, it lost contact,

158
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,150
a breaker tripped, and the ship went dark.

159
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:56,910
Finding it wasn't easy.

160
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:59,410
That cabinet held hundreds
of terminal blocks.

161
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:02,610
Investigators tested the
system for about a month before

162
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:06,110
they took the panel apart
and found the one loose wire.

163
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:09,010
In the lab, it showed
scrape marks and burn marks

164
00:07:09,060 --> 00:07:11,150
where electricity had been
jumping across the gap.

165
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,530
And there was more.

166
00:07:12,760 --> 00:07:14,250
Some of the ship's main power switches

167
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:16,570
were set to manual instead of automatic.

168
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:18,950
The NTSB found that on automatic,

169
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:21,770
the first blackout would have
lasted about ten seconds.

170
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,210
Instead, it lasted almost a minute.

171
00:07:24,420 --> 00:07:26,270
The second blackout had another cause.

172
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:28,650
The crew had been using
a pump meant for flushing

173
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:31,150
and cleaning to feed
fuel to the generators.

174
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:33,330
When the power dropped, that pump stopped,

175
00:07:33,380 --> 00:07:35,550
and it didn't restart by itself.

176
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,130
The generators ran out of fuel.

177
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:41,330
So a misplaced label, a switch
set to manual, and the wrong pump,

178
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:44,450
on the wrong night, three
ship lengths from a bridge.

179
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:46,950
Meanwhile, the Dali was
stuck under the wreckage,

180
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:49,910
with thousands of tonnes of
bridge steel lying across its bow.

181
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:51,490
And the river was blocked.

182
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,990
The Port of Baltimore
handles more cars and farm

183
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:56,870
machines than any other
port in the United States.

184
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:59,110
Now it was cut off from the sea.

185
00:07:59,460 --> 00:08:01,870
Thousands of dock workers,
truck drivers and small

186
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:05,050
businesses suddenly had
almost no work coming in.

187
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:07,550
So the salvage teams started cutting.

188
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:10,410
They brought in the largest
floating crane on the East Coast,

189
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:12,270
called the Chesapeake One Thousand,

190
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:15,210
which can lift about a
thousand tons at a time.

191
00:08:15,460 --> 00:08:18,070
And this crane has a strange past.

192
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,950
It once helped the CIA
secretly lift part of a

193
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,990
sunken Soviet submarine from
the bottom of the ocean.

194
00:08:24,140 --> 00:08:27,690
Piece by piece, they opened
small channels so a few

195
00:08:27,740 --> 00:08:30,250
ships could get in and
out, and then bigger ones.

196
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:33,330
The first small channel opened just six

197
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:36,250
days after the collapse,
for tugs and barges.

198
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:38,930
By late April, a deeper temporary channel

199
00:08:38,980 --> 00:08:41,090
let some of the biggest
ships through again.

200
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:45,410
Then, in May, they used small,
carefully placed explosive charges

201
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:48,970
to cut the biggest section of
the bridge off the Dali's bow.

202
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,950
On the twentieth of May, the Dali finally

203
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,270
floated free and was towed back into port.

204
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:58,030
And on the tenth of June, the full
shipping channel was open again.

205
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:00,870
By then, crews had
pulled an estimated fifty

206
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:03,670
thousand tons of steel and
concrete out of the river.

207
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:06,070
Most of the crew had stayed
on the ship for weeks.

208
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:09,310
Some were told to stay in
Baltimore during the investigation,

209
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:11,930
and a few were still there
almost two years later.

210
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:13,770
And the Dali itself?

211
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:17,090
After temporary repairs, it
sailed to a shipyard in China,

212
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:19,270
where its smashed bow was rebuilt.

213
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,350
In January twenty
twenty-five, about ten months

214
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:24,390
after the collapse, it went back to work,

215
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:28,170
carrying containers between
Asia and Central America.

216
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:31,970
The ship's owners agreed to pay
about one hundred and two million

217
00:09:32,020 --> 00:09:35,650
dollars for clearing the channel,
without admitting they were responsible.

218
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,530
When the Key Bridge was built
in the nineteen-seventies,

219
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:41,090
it cost about sixty million dollars.

220
00:09:41,420 --> 00:09:43,050
The new bridge will cost much more.

221
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:47,570
The latest estimate is between four point
three and five point two billion dollars,

222
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:50,510
and it's not expected to open
before late twenty thirty.

223
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:52,590
It will have a longer main span,

224
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,890
and much bigger protection
around its supports.

225
00:09:56,040 --> 00:09:59,330
And the investigation
reached far beyond Baltimore.

226
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:02,170
The NTSB asked the owners
of sixty-eight other bridges

227
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:05,390
across the United States,
including the Golden Gate Bridge,

228
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:07,870
to check how they'd survive a ship strike.

229
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:11,450
A giant ship, a bridge that
stood for almost fifty years,

230
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:14,430
and a little plastic
label in the wrong place.

231
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:18,090
Sometimes the biggest disasters
start with the smallest things.

232
00:10:18,140 --> 00:10:22,510
If you want to see another giant ship
that got stuck in a very narrow place,

233
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:27,050
and shut down a whole canal,
that video is right here.
